Letting $x=2$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(2-4)^n}{n\cdot(-2)^n}
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-2)^n}{n\cdot(-2)^n} \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{1}{n}
\end{align}$

This is the harmonic series, which is known to diverge.

Letting $x=6$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(6-4)^n}{n\cdot(-2)^n}
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{2^n}{n\cdot(-2)^n} \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-1)^n\cdot2^n}{n\cdot 2^n} \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-1)^n}{n}
\end{align}$

This is the alternating harmonic series, which is known to converge.
